INT. SAL'S TAP ROOM - 1 A.M.

Empty stools. Neon beer sign, half dead. A television mutters a weather map to nobody.

BIG LOU SALVATORE, fifties, bartender, wipes a glass that has been clean since Tuesday. At the far end of the bar sits FINN DOYLE, thirties, wearing a jacket that doesn't fit, a hat he hasn't removed, a duffel bag at his feet.

BIG LOU
(to the empty room)
Last call, folks!

Nobody answers. He sets the glass down. Pours a whiskey. Slides it to Finn.

BIG LOU (CONT'D)
You're early.

FINN
You're late.

BIG LOU
It's my bar.

FINN
It's my money.

Big Lou raises an eyebrow, polishes another glass. Finn nudges the duffel with his boot.

BIG LOU
(muttering)
Eighty grand and he wants a drink.

FINN
I heard that.

BIG LOU
You were meant to.

A car passes outside, headlights sliding across the ceiling. Both go still. The light fades.

FINN
Lou.

BIG LOU
Finn.

FINN ^
We're clean.

BIG LOU
We're clean.

They stare at each other. The weather map blinks.
